      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Student gave a confident wrong answer: describing bond formation (energy released) instead of bond energy (energy needed to break bonds), and omitted 'one mole'. Correct the misconception, provide the exact memo answer, breakdown, and exam tip.",
      "content": "That definition describes bond *formation* (an exothermic process where energy is released), not bond energy. Bond energy is an endothermic process where energy is *required* to break bonds.\n\n**Final Answer:** The energy needed to break one mole of its molecules into separate atoms.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Definition Components:** Bond energy measures the energy input required to dissociate $1\\text{ mol}$ of molecules.\n* **What the key phrases mean:**\n  * *Energy needed/required:* Bonds are stable;
 energy must be absorbed (endothermic) to pull bonded atoms apart.\n  * *One mole:* Specifies the exact molar quantity ($1\\text{ mol}$) of substance being broken.\n* **Why we use it:** It provides a standard, quantitative measure of covalent bond strength so chemists can compare how tightly different atoms hold onto each other.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Examiners are extremely strict on definition keywords! Leaving out **\"one mole\"** or stating energy is **\"released\"** will cost you both marks. Remember: *It takes energy to break bonds, and energy is released when bonds form*."
